-- statement:
--   Formal definitions for the Majorana Clifford algebra of the timepiece Lean 4 formalization (module `BookProof.MajoranaClifford`, source chapter `BookProof/ChapterMajoranaClifford.lean`).
--
--   This file formalizes the self-contained algebraic content of the book section *"Majorana spinors in canonical quantization and antiparticles"* (`book.tex` line ~7680).
--
--   There the book constructs, from a **real Hilbert space** `V` with inner product `⟪·,·⟫`, the associated **complex Clifford C\*-algebra** `C(V)`: a unital associative algebra with an injective linear map `a : V → C(V)` satisfying
--
--   * `a(v)² = ⟪v,v⟫·1` (the Clifford relation), and * `a*(v) = a(v)` (the generators are **self-adjoint** for the canonical involution `*`),
--
--   generated by the `a(v)`. The book's physical reading is that each `a(v)` is a **self-adjoint fermionic field operator** — *"a particle which is its own antiparticle"*, i.e. a Majorana fermion — and that *"it is always possible to do the canonical fermionic quantization of a real Hilbert space, with the corresponding operators `a(v)` being self-adjoint"*. The symmetries act as `a(v) → a(Tv)` for a **real** operator `T`, preserving self-adjointness, i.e. as **real representations**.
--
--   We realize `C(V)` as Mathlib's `CliffordAlgebra Q` for the quadratic form `Q v = ⟪v,v⟫` induced by the inner product, with the generator map `a v = CliffordAlgebra.ι Q v` and the canonical involution `*` given by `CliffordAlgebra.reverse` (the unique anti-automorphism fixing the generators, `reverse (a v) = a v`). We prove:
--
--   * `a_sq` — the **Clifford relation** `a(v)·a(v) = ⟪v,v⟫·1`; * `car` — the **canonical anticommutation relation** `a(v)·a(w) + a(w)·a(v) = 2⟪v,w⟫·1` (the polarization of the Clifford relation, i.e. `{a(v),a(w)} = 2⟪v,w⟫`); * `a_selfAdjoint` — `a(v)` is **self-adjoint**, `reverse (a v) = a v` (the book's `a*(v) = a(v)`), the Majorana / *own-antiparticle* property; * `reverse_star_props` (`reverse_antihom`, `reverse_involutive`) — the involution `*` is an **anti-automorphism of order 2** (the defining properties of `C(V)`'s unique `*`); * `a_map_selfAdjoint` — for **any real linear** `T : V → V`, `a(T v)` is again self-adjoint, so a real symmetry `a(v) → a(Tv)` **preserves self-adjointness** (real representation); * `car_smul_left` / `a_add` — bilinearity of `a`; * `a_anticomm_of_orthogonal` — orthogonal directions give **anticommuting** field operators, `⟪v,w⟫ = 0 ⇒ a(v)·a(w) + a(w)·a(v) = 0`; * `a_anticomm_skew` — the **creation/annihilation split**: for a skew-symmetric `J` (the complex structure `J² = -1` of the book, `⟪J v, w⟫ = -⟪v, J w⟫`), the directions `v` and `J v` are orthogonal, so `a(v)` and `a(J v)` **anticommute** — the real shadow of the CAR between the annihilation operator `a(v + iJv)` and the creation operator `a(v − iJv)`.
--
--   The C\*-completion (the natural norm) and the bosonic symplectic variant (`[a(v),a(w)] = ⟪v, J w⟫ i`, a CCR/Weyl algebra rather than a Clifford algebra) are prose in the book and are not formalized here.
